<h2 class="bde-heading-506-128 bde-heading">
Why Is Kawhi Leonard on a Maximum Deal?
</h2><div class="bde-fancy-container-506-129 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-fancy-container-506-145 bde-fancy-container">
  
  
	



<div class="bde-f-text-506-158 bde-f-text">
<p>Leonard is paid <strong>$50.3m</strong> because he has won two championships and two Finals Most Valuable Player awards with two different clubs, and the maximum is what that record buys even at 35.</p>
<ul>
<li>He has been named Defensive Player of the Year twice and selected to seven All-NBA teams, most recently the Second Team for 2025-26.</li>
<li>He won the 2014 Finals with San Antonio at 22 and the 2019 Finals with Toronto at 27, and the Raptors traded for him again in September.</li>
<li>His pay went from <strong>$2.89m</strong> to <strong>$16.5m</strong> in 2015 and has risen every year since.</li>
<li>Knee problems have cost him whole seasons, and the Clippers paid the maximum through several of them.</li>
<li>His Toronto extension starts at about <strong>$56.1m</strong> in 2027-28, about <strong>$11.1m</strong> under the maximum across its two years.</li>
</ul>
<p>He is one of a small number of players to have won the Finals award with two different clubs.</p>

